= 1F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= a
{parent=1f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <characteristic polynomial> of the given <matrix> is $(t-1)(t-2)^2$. Solving the two <eigenvalue equations> gives
$$
E_1=\operatorname{span}\{(3,-1,3)^T\},\qquad E_2=\operatorname{span}\{(2,1,0)^T,(2,0,1)^T\}.
$$
For example, multiplying the <matrix> by these three <vectors> gives respectively $1$, $2$ and $2$ times the original <vector>. The <eigenvectors> form a <basis>: the <matrix> with these columns has nonzero <determinant> $-1$. Thus **an eigenbasis and its diagonal matrix** are
$$
\boxed{\mathcal B=((3,-1,3)^T,(2,1,0)^T,(2,0,1)^T),\qquad [\alpha]_{\mathcal B}=\operatorname{diag}(1,2,2).}
$$

= b
{parent=1f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $J_r(\lambda)$ for a size-$r$ <Jordan block>. The <characteristic polynomial> fixes the total dimensions at $2$ and $-7$, while the <minimal polynomial> fixes the largest <Jordan block> at each <eigenvalue>. Consequently, the blocks for $2$ have total size four, largest size two, and at least one size-two block. The blocks for $-7$ both have size one. **The complete list, up to similarity, is**
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{diag}(J_2(2),J_2(2),-7,-7),\qquad \operatorname{diag}(J_2(2),2,2,-7,-7),\quad J_2(2)=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\0&2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
These <Jordan normal forms> are inequivalent because their $2$-<eigenspaces> have different dimensions, two and three.

= 2A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Complex Analysis or Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=2A}

At the origin, the <Taylor series> $\sin z=z+O(z^3)$ and $1-\cos z=z^2/2+O(z^4)$ give
$$
f_1(z)=z^{-3}(1+O(z)),\qquad f_2(z)=2z^{-4}(1+O(z^2)).
$$
Thus **$f_1$ has a pole of order three and $f_2$ a pole of order four at zero**. For the third function, the <Laurent series>
$$
f_3(z)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^k z^{1-2k}}{(2k+1)!}
$$
has infinitely many nonzero negative-power terms, so **zero is an essential singularity of $f_3$**.

To classify the point at infinity on the <Riemann sphere>, use the coordinate $w=1/z$. Then
$$
f_3(1/w)=w^{-2}\sin w=w^{-1}-\frac w6+O(w^3),
$$
so **$f_3$ has a simple pole at infinity**.

For the other two functions, an important qualification is necessary. The <poles> of $f_1$ at nonzero integer multiples of $\pi$, and those of $f_2$ at nonzero integer multiples of $2\pi$, approach infinity. Equivalently, their images in the $w$ coordinate accumulate at zero. Hence **infinity is a non-isolated singularity of $f_1$ and $f_2$**. Neither function is <holomorphic> on any punctured neighbourhood of infinity, so the <classification of isolated singularities> into removable singularities, poles and essential singularities does not apply there.

= 3F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry}

= a
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Poincare disc model> is the open <unit disc> $D=\{z=x+iy:|z|<1\}$ equipped with the <Riemannian metric>
$$
\boxed{ds^2=\frac{4(dx^2+dy^2)}{(1-x^2-y^2)^2}=\frac{4|dz|^2}{(1-|z|^2)^2}.}
$$
This normalization gives <Gaussian curvature> $-1$. In particular, a Euclidean curve has <hyperbolic length> $\int 2|dz|/(1-|z|^2)$; the boundary circle is infinitely far away in <hyperbolic distance>.

= b
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**A suitable <Poincare disc automorphism>** is the <Möbius transformation>
$$
\boxed{f(z)=\frac{z-a}{1-\overline a z}.}
$$
It sends $a$ to zero and has inverse $(w+a)/(1+\overline a w)$. To verify the <isometry> directly, note that
$$
1-|f(z)|^2=\frac{(1-|a|^2)(1-|z|^2)}{|1-\overline a z|^2},\qquad |f'(z)|=\frac{1-|a|^2}{|1-\overline a z|^2}.
$$
These identities show both that $f$ maps the <unit disc> onto itself and that $2|df|/(1-|f|^2)=2|dz|/(1-|z|^2)$, preserving the <Riemannian metric>.

= c
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The radial segment is a <geodesic> of the <Poincare disc model>. Its <hyperbolic length> is
$$
\boxed{d_D(0,re^{i\theta})=\int_0^r\frac{2\,dt}{1-t^2}=\log\frac{1+r}{1-r}=2\operatorname{artanh}r.}
$$
One can also verify that it minimizes <length of a curve>: along any piecewise smooth curve from zero to radius $r$, the Euclidean speed is at least the absolute radial speed. Integrating $2|dr|/(1-r^2)$ is at least the absolute change in $\log((1+r)/(1-r))$, with equality on the radial segment. Thus the answer is independent of $\theta$.

= d
{parent=3f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use an <isometry> of the <Poincare disc model> to move the centre to zero. A <hyperbolic disc> of radius $\rho$ then has Euclidean radius $R=\tanh(\rho/2)$ by the <hyperbolic distance> formula. The <area element of a surface> of the <Riemannian metric> is $4r\,dr\,d\theta/(1-r^2)^2$, so
$$
\operatorname{Area}=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^R\frac{4r}{(1-r^2)^2}\,dr\,d\theta=4\pi\left(\frac1{1-R^2}-1\right).
$$
Therefore **the area is independent of the centre**, and
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Area}=4\pi\sinh^2(\rho/2)=2\pi(\cosh\rho-1).}
$$

= 4C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Variational Principles}

= a
{parent=4c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a twice differentiable function, <convexity> on $\mathbb R^2$ is equivalent to its <Hessian matrix> being <positive semidefinite> everywhere. Here the <Hessian matrix> is constant:
$$
H=\begin{pmatrix}4&\alpha\\\alpha&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its diagonal entries are positive, so <positive semidefiniteness> is equivalent to $\det H=8-\alpha^2\geq0$. **The required parameter range is**
$$
\boxed{-2\sqrt2\leq\alpha\leq2\sqrt2.}
$$
The endpoints give <convex functions> with one flat direction; strict inequalities give a <Hessian matrix> defining a <positive-definite quadratic form> and a <strictly convex function>.

= b
{parent=4c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Completing the square in the <optimization constraint> gives
$$
\left(x_2-\frac32x_1\right)^2=\frac14x_1^2-1.
$$
The left side is nonnegative, so every feasible point has $x_1^2\geq4$. Equality is feasible precisely when $x_2=3x_1/2$ and $x_1=\pm2$. Thus **the global minimum is**
$$
\boxed{\min x_1^2=4,\qquad (x_1,x_2)=(2,3)\text{ or }(-2,-3).}
$$
There is **no maximum**: for every $|x_1|\geq2$, the values $x_2=3x_1/2\pm\sqrt{x_1^2/4-1}$ satisfy the <optimization constraint>, allowing $x_1^2$ to be arbitrarily large. This direct argument also establishes that the two stationary points are the only <local minima> on the two branches of the constraint curve.

= 5C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics}

= a
{parent=5c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Put $r^2=x^2+y^2$. On the specified domain, $r>0$, and direct differentiation gives the <divergence> and <vorticity>
$$
\nabla\cdot\mathbf u=U'(z),\qquad \nabla\times\mathbf u=\mathbf0.
$$
For example, the third component of the <curl> is $A(r^2-2x^2)/r^4+A(r^2-2y^2)/r^4=0$; the other two components vanish because $U$ depends only on $z$ and the transverse velocity is independent of $z$. Hence **the flow is both incompressible and irrotational exactly when**
$$
\boxed{U(z)=U_0\text{ is constant},\qquad A\text{ is arbitrary}.}
$$
The exclusion of the axis matters: these are ordinary derivatives on the domain, with no assertion of regularity on the removed axis.

= b
{parent=5c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In cylindrical coordinates, the transverse velocity is $A\mathbf e_\theta/r$, so its contribution to the <circulation> is $A\,d\theta$. The vertical contribution is $U(z)\,dz$, the differential of an antiderivative of $U$, and therefore has zero integral around any closed curve. If $k$ is the oriented <winding number> of the curve's projection about the origin, then
$$
\boxed{\oint\mathbf u\cdot d\mathbf r=2\pi A k.}
$$
In particular, **a single positive winding gives $2\pi A$**; reversing orientation reverses the sign. The nonzero <circulation> is compatible with <irrotational flow> because the domain is not <simply connected>. For $A\ne0$, an enclosing curve cannot bound a surface contained entirely in the domain to which <Stokes theorem> would apply.

= 6D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= a
{parent=6d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A sequence of real <orthogonal polynomials> consists of real polynomials $p_n$ with <degree of a polynomial> $n$ and
$$
\langle p_m,p_n\rangle=0\quad(m\ne n),\qquad \langle p_n,p_n\rangle>0.
$$
Orthogonality is relative to the specified <inner product>, rather than to the ordinary coefficients. A <monic polynomial> has leading coefficient one, so a <monic orthogonal polynomial> has the form $p_n(x)=x^n+\text{terms of lower degree}$. In particular, the monic degree-zero polynomial is $p_0=1$.

= b
{parent=6d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Since $xp_n-p_{n+1}$ has <degree of a polynomial> at most $n$, expand it in the <basis> $p_0,\ldots,p_n$. Multiplication by $x$ is <self-adjoint> for this weighted <inner product>: $\langle xp,q\rangle=\langle p,xq\rangle$. For $k\leq n-2$, the polynomial $xp_k$ has degree at most $n-1$, so
$$
\langle xp_n-p_{n+1},p_k\rangle=\langle p_n,xp_k\rangle=0.
$$
Only the $p_n$ and $p_{n-1}$ coefficients remain. Taking their <inner products> gives **the three-term recurrence**
$$
\boxed{p_{n+1}=(x-\alpha_n)p_n-\beta_n p_{n-1},\qquad \alpha_n=\frac{\langle xp_n,p_n\rangle}{\langle p_n,p_n\rangle},\quad \beta_n=\frac{\langle p_n,p_n\rangle}{\langle p_{n-1},p_{n-1}\rangle}>0\ (n\geq1).}
$$
For the second formula, $xp_{n-1}=p_n+$ a polynomial of lower degree, hence $\langle xp_n,p_{n-1}\rangle=\langle p_n,xp_{n-1}\rangle=\langle p_n,p_n\rangle$. At $n=0$, take $p_{-1}=0$, $\beta_0=0$ and the same formula for $\alpha_0$. Positivity of the weight ensures that all the squared <norms> in the denominators are nonzero.

= 7H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistics}

= Solution
{parent=7H}

Let $S=\sum_{i=1}^nX_i$. The <likelihood ratio> of rate two to rate one is $2^n e^{-S}$, which decreases strictly with $S$. By the <Neyman-Pearson lemma>, the **most powerful test rejects for small values of the sum**:
$$
\boxed{\text{reject if }S<c_\alpha,\qquad \mathbb P_{\lambda=1}(S<c_\alpha)=\alpha.}
$$
Under the null, $S$ has the <gamma distribution> with shape $n$ and rate one. Thus $c_\alpha$ is its $\alpha$-quantile, equivalently half the $\alpha$-quantile of the <chi-squared distribution> with $2n$ degrees of freedom. The <distribution> is continuous, so boundary randomization is unnecessary for $0<\alpha<1$.

For every fixed alternative $\lambda>1$, the <likelihood ratio> against rate one is $\lambda^n e^{-(\lambda-1)S}$, again strictly decreasing. Therefore the same region is most powerful against every such alternative. Moreover, if $G$ has the shape-$n$, rate-one <gamma distribution>, then $S$ at rate $\lambda$ has the same <distribution> as $G/\lambda$. It follows that $\mathbb P_\lambda(S<c_\alpha)=\mathbb P(G<\lambda c_\alpha)$ increases with $\lambda$, so the maximum rejection probability over $\lambda\leq1$ is $\alpha$ at one. Any competing size-$\alpha$ test for the composite null is also of level at most $\alpha$ at rate one, and is bounded in power by the <Neyman-Pearson lemma> for each alternative. **The test is uniformly most powerful for the composite hypotheses as well.** This is the <uniformly most powerful test for an exponential rate> based on the sample sum.

= 8H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization}

= Solution
{parent=8H}

Let $p$ be the row player's <mixed strategy> and $q$ the column player's <mixed strategy>. The fourth row is strictly dominated by the first. A useful candidate mixes the first two rows so that their first and third column payoffs agree. If the first row has probability $t$, these payoffs are $7t-2$ and $2-7t$, giving $t=2/7$ and common value zero.

To prove that these are <optimal mixed strategies>, use the two explicit <mixed strategies>
$$
\boxed{p=(2/7,5/7,0,0)^T,\qquad q=(1/2,0,1/2)^T,\qquad v=0.}
$$
Their payoff vectors satisfy
$$
p^TA=(0,11/7,0),\qquad Aq=(0,0,-1/2,-1)^T.
$$
Thus the row player guarantees a payoff at least zero against every column <mixed strategy>, and the column player guarantees a payoff at most zero against every row <mixed strategy>. This matching pair proves that **the value of the zero-sum game is zero**.

These <optimal mixed strategies> are also unique. Since $Aq$ is strictly negative in rows three and four, any optimal row strategy gives them probability zero. Against columns one and three it must then satisfy both $7t-2\geq0$ and $2-7t\geq0$, so $t=2/7$. Similarly, $p^TA$ is strictly positive in column two, forcing an optimal column strategy to avoid that column; its constraints against rows one and two then force equal weights on columns one and three.

= 9F
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Algebra}

= a
{parent=9f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For <matrices> $A,B$ and scalars $r,s\in\mathbb F$, the definition of the <trace> gives
$$
\operatorname{Tr}(rA+sB)=\sum_i(ra_{ii}+sb_{ii})=r\operatorname{Tr}(A)+s\operatorname{Tr}(B).
$$
Hence **the trace is a linear functional** on the <vector space> of $n\times n$ <matrices>.

= b
{parent=9f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The definition of <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
\operatorname{Tr}(AB)=\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}b_{ji}=\sum_{j,i}b_{ji}a_{ij}=\operatorname{Tr}(BA).
$$
The middle equality uses commutativity of scalar multiplication in $\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$, together with interchange of finite sums. Thus **the trace is unchanged by cyclically moving the two factors**.

= c
{parent=9f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

To prove that <trace is the unique normalized cyclic linear functional>, let $E_{ij}$ be the <matrix units>. For $i\ne j$, $E_{ii}E_{ij}=E_{ij}$ whereas $E_{ij}E_{ii}=0$, so the assumed identity for the <linear functional> gives $f(E_{ij})=0$. Also, $E_{ij}E_{ji}=E_{ii}$ and $E_{ji}E_{ij}=E_{jj}$, giving $f(E_{ii})=f(E_{jj})$. Write this common value as $c$. Since the <matrix units> form a <basis>,
$$
\boxed{f(A)=\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}f(E_{ij})=c\sum_i a_{ii}=c\operatorname{Tr}(A).}
$$
For $n=1$ the same conclusion follows immediately from the one-dimensional <basis> $E_{11}$. Finally, $f(I)=nc$; if this equals $n$, then $c=1$ over either specified field. **The normalization makes $f$ exactly the trace.**

= d
{parent=9f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Every <commutator> $[A,B]=AB-BA$ has zero <trace>, by the cyclic identity. Hence its linear span $W$ is contained in $\ker\operatorname{Tr}$. For the reverse inclusion, the <matrix units> satisfy
$$
E_{ij}=[E_{ii},E_{ij}]\quad(i\ne j),\qquad E_{ii}-E_{jj}=[E_{ij},E_{ji}].
$$
The off-diagonal <matrix units> and the differences $E_{ii}-E_{nn}$ for $i<n$ span all <traceless matrices>: a traceless diagonal part is $\sum_{i<n}a_{ii}(E_{ii}-E_{nn})$. Thus every element of the <kernel> is a linear combination of <commutators>, proving
$$
\boxed{W=\ker\operatorname{Tr}.}
$$
This establishes that <traceless matrices are spanned by commutators>. For $n=1$, both <vector subspaces> are zero. Notice that the argument needs a span of <commutators>; it does not assume that every such sum has already been written as a single commutator.

= 10E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Groups, Rings and Modules}

= a
{parent=10e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

We prove <prime avoidance> by contradiction. Remove any <prime ideal> contained in another one on the list; this leaves the same union. If the <ideal> $I$ is contained in none of the remaining <prime ideals>, choose $a_i\in I\setminus P_i$. For every $j\ne i$, incomparability lets us choose $b_{ij}\in P_j\setminus P_i$. Set
$$
b_i=\prod_{j\ne i}b_{ij},\qquad c=\sum_i a_i b_i.
$$
For a one-element list, use the empty product $b_1=1$. By the defining property of a <prime ideal>, $b_i\notin P_i$, and also $a_i b_i\notin P_i$. But $b_j\in P_i$ for every $j\ne i$. Thus all summands except $a_i b_i$ lie in $P_i$, and consequently $c\notin P_i$ for every $i$. On the other hand, $c\in I$ because each $a_i\in I$. This contradicts the assumed containment in the union. Therefore
$$
\boxed{I\subseteq P_i\text{ for at least one }i.}
$$

= b
{parent=10e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The evaluation map $\varphi:\mathbb R[x]\to\mathbb C$, $p\mapsto p(i)$, is a <ring homomorphism> and is onto because $a+bx$ maps to any $a+bi$. By <polynomial division>, write
$$
p(x)=(x^2+1)q(x)+a+bx.
$$
Then $p(i)=0$ precisely when $a=b=0$. Thus $\ker\varphi=(x^2+1)$, and the <first isomorphism theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb R[x]/(x^2+1)\cong\mathbb C.}
$$
Since the <quotient ring> is a <field>, **$(x^2+1)$ is a maximal ideal**.

= c
{parent=10e}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Evaluation at a real point defines a surjective <ring homomorphism> $p\mapsto p(a,b)$ from $\mathbb R[x,y]$ to $\mathbb R$. Its <kernel> is $(x-a,y-b)$: subtracting the constant $p(a,b)$ expresses the remainder as a multiple of $x-a$ plus a multiple of $y-b$, by successive <polynomial division>. The <first isomorphism theorem> therefore identifies the <quotient ring> with the <field> $\mathbb R$, proving **each $I_{a,b}$ is maximal**.

For a <maximal ideal> not on this list, take
$$
\boxed{J=(x^2+1,y).}
$$
Evaluation $p(x,y)\mapsto p(i,0)$ is onto $\mathbb C$, and its <kernel> is $J$: first subtract a multiple of $y$, then divide the remaining polynomial in $x$ by $x^2+1$. Hence $\mathbb R[x,y]/J\cong\mathbb C$, a <field>. Finally, if $J=I_{a,b}$ for real $a,b$, the member $x^2+1\in J$ would vanish at $(a,b)$, requiring $a^2+1=0$, which is impossible over $\mathbb R$.

= 11G
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis II}

= a
{parent=11g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <Cauchy sequence> in a <metric space> satisfies: for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $N$ such that $d(x_m,x_n)<\varepsilon$ whenever $m,n\geq N$. Suppose a <subsequence> $x_{n_k}$ converges to $x\in X$. Choose $N$ so that the Cauchy bound is $\varepsilon/2$, and then choose $k$ with $n_k\geq N$ and $d(x_{n_k},x)<\varepsilon/2$. For every $n\geq N$, the <triangle inequality> gives
$$
d(x_n,x)\leq d(x_n,x_{n_k})+d(x_{n_k},x)<\varepsilon.
$$
Thus **the whole Cauchy sequence converges to the subsequential limit**.

= b
{parent=11g}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For fixed $m$, the <reverse triangle inequality> gives
$$
|d(x_m,x_n)-d(x_m,x_k)|\leq d(x_n,x_k).
$$
The right side tends uniformly to zero as $n,k\to\infty$ because $(x_n)$ is a <Cauchy sequence>. Hence $(d(x_m,x_n))_n$ is a real <Cauchy sequence>. By <completeness> of $\mathbb R$, it has a finite, nonnegative limit:
$$
\boxed{d_m=\lim_{n\to\infty}d(x_m,x_n)\geq0.}
$$
This conclusion uses completeness only of the real line, without assuming that $X$ is complete.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Given $\varepsilon>0$, the <Cauchy sequence> property gives $N$ with $d(x_m,x_n)<\varepsilon/2$ for all $m,n\geq N$. Fixing $m\geq N$ and letting $n\to\infty$ yields $0\leq d_m\leq\varepsilon/2<\varepsilon$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{d_m\longrightarrow0.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write the <subsequence> as $y_n=x_{k_n}$ with strictly increasing indices $k_n$. If $y_\ell=x_m$, then $d(y_\ell,y_n)=d(x_m,x_{k_n})$. This is a <subsequence> of the real sequence converging to $d_m$, so
$$
\boxed{d(y_\ell,y_n)\longrightarrow d_m.}
$$
The conclusion depends on the point $x_m$ itself, even if that point appears at several indices.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Apply the <triangle inequality> with a third point $x_k$:
$$
d(x_m,x_k)-d(x_n,x_k)\leq d(x_m,x_n)\leq d(x_m,x_k)+d(x_n,x_k).
$$
Letting $k\to\infty$ gives the desired bounds. Interchanging $m,n$ also gives the sharper symmetric lower bound:
$$
\boxed{|d_m-d_n|\leq d(x_m,x_n)\leq d_m+d_n.}
$$

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

First handle possible zero values. If $d_m=d_n=0$, the previous upper bound forces $x_m=x_n$. Thus, if infinitely many $d_m$ vanish, choosing those indices gives a constant <subsequence>, which satisfies both inequalities.

Otherwise, discard the finitely many zero values and use $d_m\to0$ to choose increasing indices $k_j$ with
$$
\delta_j:=d_{k_j}>0,\qquad \delta_{j+1}\leq\delta_j/8.
$$
Set $y_j=x_{k_j}$. The preceding bounds give $|\delta_j-\delta_\ell|\leq d(y_j,y_\ell)\leq\delta_j+\delta_\ell$. For $m\geq2$,
$$
\frac{d(y_{m+1},y_m)}{d(y_m,y_{m-1})}\leq\frac{(9/8)\delta_m}{(7/8)\delta_{m-1}}\leq\frac9{56}<\frac13.
$$
For $m<n$, use $\delta_n\leq\delta_m/8$ and $\delta_{n+1}\leq\delta_m/64$ to obtain
$$
\frac{d(y_{m+1},y_{n+1})}{d(y_m,y_n)}\leq\frac{\delta_m/8+\delta_m/64}{(7/8)\delta_m}=\frac9{56}<\frac12.
$$
The same holds when $n<m$ by symmetry; when $m=n$, both distances are zero. This proves **both required inequalities**. The adjacent-distance inequality is understood for $m\geq2$, since this <subsequence> starts at index one and has no $y_0$.

= c
{parent=11g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

We argue by contradiction using the previous <subsequence> construction. If $X$ is not <complete>, choose a <Cauchy sequence> with no limit in $X$. None of its $d_m$ can be zero: if $d_m=0$, then $d(x_m,x_n)\to0$, giving a limit in $X$. The construction therefore produces pairwise distinct $y_j$ with
$$
d(y_{m+1},y_{n+1})\leq\tfrac12d(y_m,y_n).
$$
Let $Y=\{y_1,y_2,\ldots\}$. **This subset is closed in $X$.** Indeed, if a point $x\notin Y$ lay in its <closure>, there would be points of $Y$ tending to $x$. Their indices must be unbounded, because the distance from $x$ to any finite subset of $Y$ is positive. Passing to increasing indices gives a <convergent subsequence> of the original <Cauchy sequence>. Part (a) would then make the whole sequence converge to $x$, a contradiction.

Now define $F:Y\to Y$ by $F(y_j)=y_{j+1}$. Distinctness makes this well-defined, and the displayed inequality makes it a <contraction mapping> with constant at most $1/2$. It has no <fixed point>, contradicting the assumed property of closed subsets. Consequently
$$
\boxed{X\text{ is complete}.}
$$
This is the converse direction of <contraction property of closed subsets characterizes completeness>.

= 12E
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Metric and Topological Spaces}

= Solution
{parent=12E}

For $q\in\mathbb Q\setminus\{0\}$ write $q=p^k u/v$, where $k\in\mathbb Z$ and neither integer $u$ nor integer $v$ is divisible by $p$. Define the <p-adic valuation> $v_p(q)=k$ and the <p-adic absolute value> $|q|_p=p^{-k}$, with $|0|_p=0$. **The p-adic metric** is
$$
\boxed{d_p(a,b)=|a-b|_p.}
$$
Factoring out the smaller power of $p$ from two rational numbers shows $v_p(q+r)\geq\min\{v_p(q),v_p(r)\}$, with the usual convention $v_p(0)=+\infty$. Consequently the <ultrametric inequality> gives
$$
d_p(a,b)=|(a-c)+(c-b)|_p\leq\max\{d_p(a,c),d_p(c,b)\}.
$$
Together with symmetry, positivity and separation of points, this verifies that $d_p$ is a <metric>.

For the given geometric partial sums, use the finite <geometric series> identity. Since $p$ does not divide $p-1$,
$$
a_n=\frac{p^n-1}{p-1},\qquad \left|a_n+\frac1{p-1}\right|_p=\left|\frac{p^n}{p-1}\right|_p=p^{-n}\longrightarrow0.
$$
Thus **the limit is the rational number** $\boxed{1/(1-p)}$, even though the partial sums diverge in the ordinary real <metric>.

If $|a|_p>|b|_p$, the <ultrametric inequality> gives $|a+b|_p\leq|a|_p$. Applying it to $a=(a+b)-b$ gives $|a|_p\leq\max\{|a+b|_p,|b|_p\}$. Since $|b|_p<|a|_p$, this forces $|a+b|_p=|a|_p$. Interchanging $a,b$ handles the other unequal case, proving
$$
\boxed{|a+b|_p=\max\{|a|_p,|b|_p\}\quad\text{when }|a|_p\ne|b|_p.}
$$
Finally, let $b$ be outside the <open ball> $B(a,\delta)$, so $d_p(a,b)\geq\delta$. If $d_p(b,c)<\delta$, the preceding unequal-norm identity gives $d_p(a,c)=d_p(a,b)\geq\delta$. Therefore $B(b,\delta)$ also lies outside $B(a,\delta)$. Its complement is <open>, so **every p-adic open ball is also closed**, that is, a <clopen set>.

= 13A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Complex Analysis or Complex Methods}

= a
{parent=13a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Take the <principal square root> $w=u+iv$, which has $u>0$ on the slit domain. The inverse relation $z=w^2$ gives $x=u^2-v^2$ and $y=2uv$. Hence the defining inequality for the parabolic domain becomes
$$
y^2<4(1-x)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad (u^2-1)(1+v^2)<0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad u<1.
$$
Thus **the image is the strip** $\boxed{0<\operatorname{Re}w<1}$. Conversely, every point of this strip squares to a point of the parabolic domain outside the removed nonpositive real axis, and its <principal square root> is the original $w$. This proves that the map is onto the strip and one-to-one. Its derivative $1/(2\sqrt z)$ is nonzero there, so it is a <conformal map>.

= b
{parent=13a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

It helps to separate the <tangent> and the squaring map. Put $t=\tan(z/2)$, with $z=x+iy$. The identity
$$
t=\frac{\sin x+i\sinh y}{\cos x+\cosh y},\qquad |t|^2=\frac{\cosh y-\cos x}{\cosh y+\cos x}
$$
shows that $0<x<\pi/2$ gives $\operatorname{Re}t>0$ and $|t|<1$. To see that every point of this right half-disc occurs, set
$$
R=\frac{1+it}{1-it},\qquad \operatorname{Re}R=\frac{1-|t|^2}{|1-it|^2}>0,\qquad \operatorname{Im}R=\frac{2\operatorname{Re}t}{|1-it|^2}>0.
$$
Then $z=-i\operatorname{Log}R$ has $0<\operatorname{Re}z<\pi/2$ and satisfies $\tan(z/2)=t$. The <principal complex logarithm> is unambiguous because $R$ is in the first quadrant.

Squaring maps this right half-disc bijectively onto the <unit disc> with the interval $(-1,0]$ removed: the argument of $t$ lies between $-\pi/2$ and $\pi/2$, and doubles into $(-\pi,\pi)$. Thus **the desired image is**
$$
\boxed{\{w:|w|<1\}\setminus(-1,0].}
$$
Neither derivative vanishes in the domain, so the composition is also a <conformal map> onto that slit disc.

= c
{parent=13a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

On the parabolic domain with the nonpositive real axis removed, first apply the <principal square root>, then scale by $\pi/2$, and then apply the map of part (b). This gives the stated composite $C(z)$ and maps bijectively onto the slit <unit disc>.

There is no genuine <branch cut> in the composite. The function $\tan^2(\pi s/4)$ is even in $s$, so changing the local square root has no effect. Near zero its <Taylor series> starts as
$$
\tan^2(\pi\sqrt z/4)=\frac{\pi^2}{16}z+O(z^2),
$$
giving a <holomorphic> extension with $C(0)=0$. There are no <poles> within the parabolic domain: the possible squared locations are $z=(2+4k)^2$, all on the excluded real portion $x\geq4$.

For $z=-r^2$ with $r\geq0$, the continuation gives
$$
C(-r^2)=-\tanh^2(\pi r/4).
$$
As $r$ ranges from zero to infinity, these values run once through $(-1,0]$. They fill precisely the slit omitted by the previous mapping, and no point away from the negative real axis has an image in that slit. Therefore **the extended map is a bijection onto the whole unit disc**:
$$
\boxed{C:\{x+iy:y^2<4(1-x)\}\longrightarrow\{w:|w|<1\}.}
$$
It is <holomorphic> throughout, so this bijection is the desired <conformal map from a parabolic domain to the unit disc>; in particular $C'(0)=\pi^2/16\ne0$.

= 14A
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Methods}

= a
{parent=14a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the real coefficients of the stated <self-adjoint> <Sturm-Liouville problem>, define the weighted <inner product>
$$
\boxed{\langle u,v\rangle_w=\int_a^b w(x)\overline{u(x)}v(x)\,dx.}
$$
Since $w>0$, a nonzero eigenfunction has positive squared <norm>. Let $L y=-(py')'+qy$. <Integration by parts> and the <Dirichlet boundary conditions> give
$$
\int_a^b\overline u\,Lv\,dx=\int_a^b\bigl(p\overline{u'}v'+q\overline uv\bigr)\,dx=\int_a^b\overline{Lu}\,v\,dx.
$$
Taking $u=v$ to be an <eigenfunction> shows that its <eigenvalue> is real, since $\lambda\int w|u|^2=\int(p|u'|^2+q|u|^2)$ is real. For two eigenfunctions with <eigenvalues> $\lambda,\mu$, the same identity gives
$$
(\mu-\lambda)\langle u,v\rangle_w=0.
$$
Hence **distinct eigenvalues imply orthogonality in the weighted inner product**. This is the <orthogonality of Sturm-Liouville eigenfunctions>. The calculation assumes the regularity needed for the stated self-adjoint boundary problem, so all boundary terms and integrals are defined.

= b
{parent=14a}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply the equation by $x^2$ and combine the derivative terms:
$$
(x^3y')'+(1+\lambda)xy=0.
$$
Thus **the self-adjoint form** is
$$
\boxed{-(x^3y')'-xy=\lambda xy,\qquad p=x^3,\quad q=-x,\quad w=x.}
$$
The <Dirichlet boundary conditions> remain at $1$ and $e$, and the weight $w=x$ is positive on this interval, as required for the <Sturm-Liouville problem>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Set $s=\log x$ and $Y(s)=y(e^s)$, so $0\leq s\leq1$. By the <chain rule>, the equation becomes $Y''+2Y'+(1+\lambda)Y=0$. The substitution $Y=e^{-s}v$ reduces it further to
$$
v''+\lambda v=0,\qquad v(0)=v(1)=0.
$$
Multiplication by $\overline v$ and <integration by parts> gives $\lambda\int_0^1|v|^2=\int_0^1|v'|^2>0$ for a nonzero solution, ruling out zero and negative <eigenvalues>. For $\lambda=k^2>0$, the first boundary condition gives $v=A\sin(ks)$, and the second gives $k=n\pi$, $n=1,2,\ldots$. Thus **all eigenpairs are**
$$
\boxed{\lambda_n=n^2\pi^2,\qquad y_n(x)=\frac1x\sin(n\pi\log x),\quad n\geq1,}
$$
with arbitrary nonzero scalar multiples of each <eigenfunction>. These are the complete list since all solutions of the constant-coefficient <differential equation> have been considered; the transformed sine system is also complete in the corresponding weighted <Hilbert space>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For the weight $w=x$, substitution $x=e^s$ gives
$$
\langle y_m,y_n\rangle_w=\int_1^e\frac{\sin(m\pi\log x)\sin(n\pi\log x)}x\,dx=\int_0^1\sin(m\pi s)\sin(n\pi s)\,ds.
$$
The <product-to-sum formula> makes this zero when $m\ne n$ and $1/2$ when $m=n$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\langle y_m,y_n\rangle_w=\tfrac12\delta_{mn}.}
$$
In particular the <eigenfunctions> are <orthogonal>, and multiplying each by $\sqrt2$ gives an <orthonormal basis> for the weighted space.

= 15B
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Quantum Mechanics}

= a
{parent=15b}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the normalized <eigenfunctions> of the <infinite square well>,
$$
\phi_n(x)=\sqrt{\frac2a}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{a},\qquad E_n=\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2},\quad 0<x<a.
$$
The sine identity expands the initial <wavefunction> as
$$
\psi_0=\frac{3\phi_1-\phi_3}{\sqrt{10}}.
$$
The normalization factor $4/\sqrt{5a}$ yields precisely these coefficients. By the <Born rule>, the <energy measurement> probabilities are $9/10$ for $E_1$ and $1/10$ for $E_3=9E_1$. Thus **the energy expectation is**
$$
\boxed{\langle E\rangle=\frac9{10}E_1+\frac1{10}E_3=\frac{9\pi^2\hbar^2}{10ma^2}.}
$$
The two probabilities sum to one, also checking the <wavefunction normalization>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Each <energy eigenstate> acquires the phase $e^{-iE_nt/\hbar}$ under <unitary time evolution>. Consequently **at the requested time $t=1$**,
$$
\boxed{\psi(x,1)=\frac1{\sqrt{10}}\left(3e^{-iE_1/\hbar}\phi_1(x)-e^{-i9E_1/\hbar}\phi_3(x)\right),\qquad 0<x<a,}
$$
and the <wavefunction> is zero outside the well. Equivalently, in elementary functions,
$$
\psi(x,1)=\frac1{\sqrt{5a}}\left(3e^{-i\pi^2\hbar/(2ma^2)}\sin\frac{\pi x}{a}-e^{-i9\pi^2\hbar/(2ma^2)}\sin\frac{3\pi x}{a}\right).
$$
The distinct phases retain their relative sign from the initial expansion; an overall <global phase> would not change measurement probabilities.

= b
{parent=15b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <bound state>, $0<E<U_0$: the <Hamiltonian operator> is nonnegative, and an energy at or above the exterior potential does not give a nonzero <normalizable wavefunction>. The <time-independent Schrodinger equation> and the hard wall at zero therefore give
$$
\psi(x)=A\sin(kx)\quad(0<x<a),\qquad \psi(x)=B e^{-\kappa(x-a)}\quad(x>a),
\qquad k=\frac{\sqrt{2mE}}{\hbar},\quad \kappa=\frac{\sqrt{2m(U_0-E)}}{\hbar}.
$$
Continuity of the <wavefunction> and its derivative at the finite step gives $k\cot(ka)=-\kappa$. Put $z=ka$ and $z_0=a\sqrt{2mU_0}/\hbar$. The matching equation becomes
$$
z\cot z=-\sqrt{z_0^2-z^2},\qquad 0<z<z_0.
$$
It requires $\cot z<0$, so solutions lie in the intervals $((j+1/2)\pi,(j+1)\pi)$ for $j=0,1,\ldots$. Squaring, with that sign restriction retained, gives $z_0=z/|\sin z|$. On each such interval this function increases strictly from $(j+1/2)\pi$ to infinity: its derivative is $(|\sin z|-z\,\operatorname{sgn}(\sin z)\cos z)/\sin^2z>0$, because the second term in the numerator is positive there. Each interval therefore contributes exactly one <bound state> if and only if $z_0>(j+1/2)\pi$. These are the <bound-state thresholds for a square well with one hard wall>.

**Exactly one normalizable bound state exists if and only if**
$$
\boxed{\frac{\pi^2\hbar^2}{8ma^2}<U_0\leq\frac{9\pi^2\hbar^2}{8ma^2}.}
$$
**The printed strict upper inequality misses the threshold case.** At the upper endpoint, the prospective second state has $E=U_0$ and $\kappa=0$. Its exterior solution is constant or linear, hence is not square-integrable unless zero; the zero exterior solution and matching would force the entire solution to vanish. Thus there is still exactly one <bound state> at that endpoint. The strict upper inequality is valid if threshold values are additionally excluded, but that exclusion is not part of the stated assumptions.

= 16D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Electromagnetism}

= a
{parent=16d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In <electrostatics>, the relevant <Maxwell equations> reduce to $\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\rho/\varepsilon_0$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf E=0$. For a volume $V$ with outward unit normal $\mathbf n$, integrate the first equation and apply the <divergence theorem>:
$$
\boxed{\oint_{\partial V}\mathbf E\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=\int_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf E\,dV=\frac1{\varepsilon_0}\int_V\rho\,dV=\frac{Q_V}{\varepsilon_0}.}
$$
This is **the integral form of Gauss's law**, with $Q_V$ the enclosed <electric charge>. The zero <curl> is the accompanying condition allowing an <electrostatic potential> locally, and globally when the domain permits one.

= b
{parent=16d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

By cylindrical symmetry the <electric field> of an infinite <line charge> is radial, independent of $z$ and angle. Use a coaxial cylindrical <Gaussian surface> of radius $r$ and length $L$. The end caps have zero flux, so <Gauss's law> gives $2\pi rL E_r=\mu L/\varepsilon_0$. Hence **the field and electrostatic potential are**
$$
\boxed{\mathbf E=\frac{\mu}{2\pi\varepsilon_0r}\mathbf e_r,\qquad \phi(r)=-\frac{\mu}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\log\frac r{r_0}+C.}
$$
Here $r_0>0$ is an arbitrary reference length, and $\mathbf E=-\nabla\phi$. One cannot choose zero <electrostatic potential> at infinity for a single infinite <line charge>, because its logarithmic potential diverges there; the arbitrary reference handles the additive constant.

= c
{parent=16d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the <conductor in electrostatic equilibrium>, the interior <electric field> is zero and the <electrostatic potential> is constant. Earthing fixes that constant to zero. Thus **the conductor boundary conditions** at $x=d$ are
$$
\boxed{\phi(d,y,z)=0,\qquad E_y(d^-,y,z)=E_z(d^-,y,z)=0.}
$$
The normal <electric field> need not vanish; it is supported by a <surface charge density>. The outward normal from this conductor into the exterior is $\mathbf n=-\mathbf e_x$, so the normal jump condition is
$$
\mathbf E(d^-)\cdot\mathbf n=\frac\sigma{\varepsilon_0},\qquad E_x(d^-)=-\frac\sigma{\varepsilon_0}.
$$
This sign convention will determine the sign of the induced <surface charge density> below.

= d
{parent=16d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <method of images> uses the real <line charge> at the origin and the opposite <image charge> at $(2d,0)$. Let
$$
r_1=\sqrt{x^2+y^2},\qquad r_2=\sqrt{(x-2d)^2+y^2}.
$$
Superposing their logarithmic <electrostatic potentials> gives, in the physical region $x<d$,
$$
\boxed{\phi(x,y)=\frac{\mu}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\log\frac{r_2}{r_1}.}
$$
At $x=d$, $r_1=r_2$, so $\phi=0$. Its tangential derivatives also vanish on that plane, giving $E_y=E_z=0$, exactly the <electrostatic boundary conditions at a conductor>. The image lies outside the physical domain, so it creates no extra physical source there. Together with the appropriate behaviour at infinity, <uniqueness of Poisson equation> identifies this field with the grounded-conductor solution.

= e
{parent=16d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <force> on the real <line charge> is due to the induced conductor field, represented by the <image charge>; its own singular field must be omitted. The opposite image at distance $2d$ produces at the origin a field toward the conductor,
$$
\mathbf E_{\rm image}(0)=\frac{\mu}{4\pi\varepsilon_0d}\mathbf e_x.
$$
Multiplying by the charge per unit length gives **the attractive force per unit length**:
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mathbf F}{L}=\frac{\mu^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0d}\mathbf e_x.}
$$
The attraction holds for either sign of $\mu$. The geometry assumes $d>0$, so the real line lies outside the conductor.

= f
{parent=16d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Differentiating the <image charge> potential gives
$$
E_x=\frac{\mu}{2\pi\varepsilon_0}\left(\frac{x}{x^2+y^2}-\frac{x-2d}{(x-2d)^2+y^2}\right).
$$
At the conducting plane, $E_x(d^-)=\mu d/[\pi\varepsilon_0(d^2+y^2)]$. With outward conductor normal $-\mathbf e_x$, the <surface charge density> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\sigma(y,z)=-\varepsilon_0 E_x(d^-,y,z)=-\frac{\mu d}{\pi(d^2+y^2)}.}
$$
It is independent of $z$ by translational symmetry. As a check, integrating across the plane gives $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\sigma(y,z)\,dy=-\mu$, the total induced charge per unit length, opposite to the real <line charge>.

= 17C
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics}

= a
{parent=17c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\boldsymbol\omega=\nabla\times\mathbf u$ be the <vorticity>. In component notation, contracting two <Levi-Civita symbols> gives
$$
(\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega)_i=\varepsilon_{ijk}u_j\varepsilon_{klm}\partial_lu_m=u_j\partial_i u_j-u_j\partial_j u_i.
$$
The first term is $\partial_i(|\mathbf u|^2/2)$ and the second is the $i$th component of the <convective acceleration>. Rearranging proves **the velocity identity**
$$
\boxed{(\mathbf u\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u=\nabla\left(\tfrac12|\mathbf u|^2\right)-\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega.}
$$

= b
{parent=17c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Parametrize a <streamline> by $\mathbf r(s)$ with $d\mathbf r/ds=\mathbf u(\mathbf r(s))$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
\frac d{ds}H(\mathbf r(s))=\nabla H\cdot\frac{d\mathbf r}{ds}=\mathbf u\cdot\nabla H=0.
$$
Therefore **$H$ is constant along every streamline**. A different regular parametrization merely multiplies this derivative by a scalar and gives the same conclusion. For time-dependent flow, the claim concerns streamlines at a fixed time, rather than <Lagrangian trajectories>.

= c
{parent=17c}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write the conservative body force per unit mass as $-\nabla\Phi$. For a fluid of constant density $\rho$, the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> and the <incompressible flow> condition are
$$
\partial_t\mathbf u+(\mathbf u\cdot\nabla)\mathbf u=-\nabla\left(\frac p\rho+\Phi\right),\qquad \nabla\cdot\mathbf u=0.
$$
Using part (a), these become
$$
\partial_t\mathbf u-\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega=-\nabla H,\qquad H=\tfrac12|\mathbf u|^2+\frac p\rho+\Phi.
$$
For <irrotational flow>, locally choose a <velocity potential> with $\mathbf u=\nabla\phi$. Then $\nabla(\partial_t\phi+H)=0$, so **the unsteady Bernoulli integral** is
$$
\boxed{\partial_t\phi+\tfrac12|\mathbf u|^2+\frac p\rho+\Phi=C(t).}
$$
The constant is spatially uniform on a connected region where the <velocity potential> is defined; its time dependence can be absorbed into the additive time-dependent gauge of $\phi$. A global single-valued <velocity potential> requires an appropriate topological condition, such as a <simply connected> domain; without it, the integral is locally valid. In a connected steady irrotational region, $\nabla H=0$ directly gives one spatial Bernoulli constant even without choosing a global potential.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

In <steady flow>, the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> and part (a) give
$$
\nabla H=\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega,\qquad H=\tfrac12|\mathbf u|^2+\frac p\rho+\Phi.
$$
Taking the dot product with $\mathbf u$ yields $\mathbf u\cdot\nabla H=0$, because a <cross product> is perpendicular to its first factor. Part (b) therefore gives **Bernoulli's quantity along streamlines**:
$$
\boxed{\tfrac12|\mathbf u|^2+\frac p\rho+\Phi=\text{constant on each streamline}.}
$$
For rotational flow these constants can differ between <streamlines>, since $\mathbf u\times\boldsymbol\omega$ need not vanish and therefore $\nabla H$ need not be zero. Special rotational flows can still have one global constant when that cross product vanishes; the general conclusion is only constancy along individual <streamlines>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $\mathbf v$ denote the velocity relative to the uniformly rotating frame, and write $r_\perp^2=x^2+y^2$. For rotation $\boldsymbol\Omega=\Omega\mathbf e_z$, the <Euler equations for an inviscid fluid> in that frame are
$$
\partial_t\mathbf v+(\mathbf v\cdot\nabla)\mathbf v+2\boldsymbol\Omega\times\mathbf v=-\nabla\left(\frac p\rho+\Phi-\tfrac12\Omega^2r_\perp^2\right).
$$
The negative quadratic term is the <centrifugal potential>, whose negative gradient is the outward <centrifugal acceleration> per unit mass. In <steady flow>, dotting the equation with $\mathbf v$ eliminates the <Coriolis force>, since $\mathbf v\cdot(\boldsymbol\Omega\times\mathbf v)=0$. The remaining acceleration term is $\mathbf v\cdot\nabla(|\mathbf v|^2/2)$. Thus
$$
\mathbf v\cdot\nabla\left(\tfrac12|\mathbf v|^2+\frac p\rho+\Phi-\tfrac12\Omega^2r_\perp^2\right)=0.
$$
By part (b), **the <steady rotating-frame Bernoulli integral> is**
$$
\boxed{\tfrac12|\mathbf v|^2+\frac p\rho+\Phi-\tfrac12\Omega^2(x^2+y^2)=\text{constant on each relative streamline}.}
$$
Steadiness here is measured in the rotating frame, including the body-force potential used in that frame.

= 18D
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= a
{parent=18d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Convergence of a numerical method> on a fixed interval $[0,T]$ means that, as the step size tends to zero and the starting approximations tend to the exact initial values,
$$
\boxed{\max_{0\leq t_n\leq T}|y_n-y(t_n)|\longrightarrow0.}
$$
For a vector-valued <ordinary differential equation>, replace the absolute value by a <norm>. The interval remains fixed as $h\to0$; a small error at only one step is insufficient.

The <order of a numerical method> is measured by its defect on a smooth exact solution. With the unscaled residual convention for a <linear multistep method>, order at least $p$ means local residual $O(h^{p+1})$; equivalently, the residual divided by $h$ is $O(h^p)$. Order exactly $p$ means the next coefficient does not vanish in general. For a <zero-stable> method, under the usual smoothness and <Lipschitz continuity> assumptions and with starting errors $O(h^p)$, this yields <global error> $O(h^p)$ on the fixed interval. Local order by itself does not imply convergence without stability.

= b
{parent=18d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Insert an exact solution into the <linear multistep method> and use $f(t,y(t))=y'(t)$. Its local residual is
$$
R_h(t)=\sum_{\ell=0}^s\rho_\ell y(t+\ell h)-h\sum_{\ell=0}^s\sigma_\ell y'(t+\ell h).
$$
Expanding both terms by <Taylor theorem> gives
$$
R_h(t)=C_0y(t)+\sum_{k=1}^p\frac{h^k}{k!}C_k y^{(k)}(t)+O(h^{p+1}),\qquad C_0=\sum_\ell\rho_\ell,\quad C_k=\sum_\ell\ell^k\rho_\ell-k\sum_\ell\ell^{k-1}\sigma_\ell.
$$
Consequently $C_0=\cdots=C_p=0$ is sufficient for order at least $p$. It is also necessary: test the residual on the polynomials $y(t)=1,t,\ldots,t^p$, evaluating at $t=0$. For $y(t)=t^k$, it equals $h^k C_k$ (and equals $C_0$ for the constant), which can be $O(h^{p+1})$ only if $C_k=0$. These functions can be realized as exact solutions of suitable smooth <ordinary differential equations>, so this is a genuine necessity test. Thus **the order conditions are**
$$
\boxed{\sum_{\ell=0}^s\rho_\ell=0,\qquad \sum_{\ell=0}^s\ell^k\rho_\ell=k\sum_{\ell=0}^s\ell^{k-1}\sigma_\ell\quad(1\leq k\leq p).}
$$
This is a statement about local order; <zero-stability> is the separate condition that turns it into convergent approximation.

= c
{parent=18d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <Dahlquist equivalence theorem> states that a <linear multistep method> has <convergence of a numerical method> **if and only if it is consistent and zero-stable**, under the standard assumptions for the <initial value problem> and convergent starting values. <Consistency of a numerical method> means order at least one, or
$$
\rho(1)=0,\qquad \rho'(1)=\sigma(1),\quad \rho(\zeta)=\sum_{\ell=0}^s\rho_\ell\zeta^\ell,\quad \sigma(\zeta)=\sum_{\ell=0}^s\sigma_\ell\zeta^\ell.
$$
The <root condition for a multistep method> expresses <zero-stability>: every root of $\rho$ has modulus at most one, and every root with modulus one is simple. Usual hypotheses include a well-posed <ordinary differential equation> with $f$ continuous in time and <Lipschitz continuous> in the solution variable, a valid step equation, and starting approximations converging to the exact values.

= d
{parent=18d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

With $\rho_0=a$, $\rho_1=\theta$, $\rho_2=1$, the constant order condition gives $a=-1-\theta$. The next three <order conditions for a linear multistep method> give
$$
\theta+2=\sigma_0+\sigma_1+\sigma_2,\qquad \theta+4=2\sigma_1+4\sigma_2,\qquad \theta+8=3\sigma_1+12\sigma_2.
$$
Solving this linear system yields **the unique coefficients of order at least three**:
$$
\boxed{a=-1-\theta,\qquad \sigma_0=\frac{5\theta+4}{12},\quad \sigma_1=\frac{2(\theta+2)}3,\quad \sigma_2=\frac{4-\theta}{12}.}
$$
The characteristic polynomial controlling <zero-stability> factors as
$$
\rho(\zeta)=\zeta^2+\theta\zeta-1-\theta=(\zeta-1)(\zeta+1+\theta).
$$
The other root is $-1-\theta$. Requiring its modulus to be at most one gives $-2\leq\theta\leq0$, but $\theta=-2$ produces a repeated root at one and violates the <root condition for a multistep method>. At $\theta=0$, the roots $1,-1$ are both simple, so that endpoint is allowed. By the <Dahlquist equivalence theorem>, **the method converges precisely for**
$$
\boxed{-2<\theta\leq0.}
$$
The fourth defect coefficient is $C_4=\theta$, so the method has order exactly three for nonzero $\theta$. At $\theta=0$ it is the two-step Simpson method, of order four; thus the extra-order endpoint has correctly remained in the answer.

= 19H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Statistics}

= a
{parent=19h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <statistic> $T$ is a <sufficient statistic> for $\theta$ if the conditional <distribution> of the data given $T$ does not depend on $\theta$. For a dominated family with density or mass function $f_\theta(x)$, the <Fisher-Neyman factorization theorem> states that this is equivalent to
$$
\boxed{f_\theta(x)=g_\theta(T(x))h(x),}
$$
where $h$ is nonnegative and independent of $\theta$, and $g_\theta$ is nonnegative and depends on the data only through $T$.

For a discrete sample space, suppose first that the factorization holds. If $\mathbb P_\theta(T=t)>0$, summing over the fibre gives $\mathbb P_\theta(T=t)=g_\theta(t)\sum_{T(x)=t}h(x)$, so
$$
\mathbb P_\theta(X=x\mid T=t)=\frac{h(x)}{\sum_{T(z)=t}h(z)}\quad(T(x)=t).
$$
This conditional <distribution> is independent of $\theta$, proving that $T$ is a <sufficient statistic>. Conversely, let $q_t(x)$ be the common conditional mass function guaranteed by the <sufficient statistic> property, for every fibre attainable under at least one parameter. Set $h(x)=q_{T(x)}(x)$ and $g_\theta(t)=\mathbb P_\theta(T=t)$. Then $\mathbb P_\theta(X=x)=g_\theta(T(x))h(x)$. Fibres never attained under any parameter can be assigned any conditional distribution, since their multiplying probability is always zero. This proves both directions of the discrete criterion, including possible parameter-dependent supports.

= b
{parent=19h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Rao-Blackwell theorem> says that an unbiased finite-variance <estimator> $\delta(X)$ of a parameter function $\tau(\theta)$ can be improved using a <sufficient statistic> $T$. Define
$$
\delta^*(T)=\mathbb E[\delta(X)\mid T].
$$
Because the conditional <distribution> given $T$ is parameter-independent, this is a statistic computable without knowing $\theta$. The <tower property of conditional expectation> gives $\mathbb E_\theta\delta^*=\mathbb E_\theta\delta=\tau(\theta)$, so it remains unbiased. The <law of total variance> gives
$$
\operatorname{Var}_\theta(\delta)=\operatorname{Var}_\theta(\delta^*)+\mathbb E_\theta[\operatorname{Var}_\theta(\delta\mid T)].
$$
Hence **the improved estimator has no larger variance or mean squared error**:
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}_\theta(\delta^*)\leq\operatorname{Var}_\theta(\delta),\qquad \mathbb E_\theta[(\delta^*-\tau(\theta))^2]\leq\mathbb E_\theta[(\delta-\tau(\theta))^2].}
$$
Equality holds precisely when $\delta=\delta^*(T)$ almost surely under the relevant parameter. More generally, even without unbiasedness, <conditional Jensen inequality> shows that conditioning on a sufficient statistic cannot increase risk for any convex loss in the estimate, provided the expectations exist. The squared-loss result above is the usual variance form of the theorem.

= c
{parent=19h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Write $L=\min_iX_i$, $R=\max_iX_i$ and $M=\max_i|X_i|=\max\{-L,R\}$. The joint <probability density> factors as
$$
f_\theta(x)=(2\theta)^{-n}\mathbf1_{\{\theta\geq\max_i|x_i|\}}=(2\theta)^{-n}\mathbf1_{\{\theta\geq\max(-L,R)\}}.
$$
By the <Fisher-Neyman factorization theorem>, **$T=(L,R)$ is a <sufficient statistic>**. The same factorization shows that the smaller <statistic> $M$ is also a <sufficient statistic>. This gives the <sufficient statistic for a symmetric uniform sample>.

In fact, the <likelihood-ratio criterion for minimal sufficiency> identifies $M$ as a <minimal sufficient statistic>: two sample points with the same $M$ have identical likelihoods for all $\theta$; with different $M$, their likelihoods have different parameter supports, and so are not proportional as functions of $\theta$. Equivalently, the condition that $f_\theta(x)=c f_\theta(y)$ for every $\theta$ and some positive constant $c$ holds exactly when their absolute maxima agree.

To see rigorously that $T$ cannot be recovered from $M$ almost surely, fix any $\theta>0$. The sample has a <distribution> invariant under simultaneous sign reversal. If $T=h(M)$ almost surely, the same identity would hold for the sign-reversed sample on another probability-one set. Since $M$ is unchanged by sign reversal, it would follow that
$$
(L,R)=(-R,-L)\quad\text{almost surely},
$$
and hence $L=-R$ almost surely. But this event has probability zero for a finite sample from a continuous <uniform distribution>: it entails $X_i=-X_j$ for some pair of indices, including the possible case $X_i=0$. Therefore $T$ is not a function of the sufficient statistic $M$. **The pair of extrema is not minimally sufficient**, for every $n\geq1$, even though it is sufficient.

= 20H
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}
{title2=Markov Chains}

= a
{parent=20h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <Markov chain> is <irreducible> if every state can reach every other state in finitely many steps with positive probability. It is a <recurrent Markov chain> if, starting at any state, it returns to that state with probability one; for an irreducible chain this property is shared by all states.

For the <simple symmetric random walk> on $\mathbb Z$, taking $|j-i|$ steps all in the appropriate direction reaches $j$ from $i$ with probability $2^{-|j-i|}>0$. Thus the chain is irreducible. Its return probabilities are
$$
p_{00}^{(2k)}=\binom{2k}{k}2^{-2k},\qquad p_{00}^{(2k+1)}=0.
$$
By <Stirling formula>, $p_{00}^{(2k)}\sim1/\sqrt{\pi k}$, so their sum diverges. The standard recurrence criterion $\sum_{n\geq0}p_{00}^{(n)}=\infty$ therefore proves **the walk is recurrent**.

The printed hint's constant is erroneous: the actual limit of $\sqrt{k}\,p_{00}^{(2k)}$ is $1/\sqrt\pi$. Its positivity is what the recurrence argument requires, but the reciprocal is necessary for the correct numerical value.

= b
{parent=20h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

An irreducible <Markov chain> has <positive recurrent states> if the expected first return time to a state is finite; this then holds for every state. A recurrent chain with infinite mean return time has <null recurrent states>.

An irreducible positive recurrent chain admits a <stationary distribution>. If such a distribution $\pi$ existed for this walk, its stationarity equations would give
$$
\pi_j=\tfrac12(\pi_{j-1}+\pi_{j+1})\quad(j\in\mathbb Z).
$$
Thus $\pi_{j+1}-\pi_j=\pi_j-\pi_{j-1}$, so $\pi_j=A+Bj$. Nonnegativity for all positive and negative integers forces $B=0$. A constant sequence cannot sum to one over $\mathbb Z$, so no stationary probability distribution exists. Hence **the walk is null recurrent, not positive recurrent**: all its states are <null recurrent states>. The first-return generating function in part (c) also verifies directly that its mean return time is infinite.

= c
{parent=20h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $u_n=\mathbb P_0(X_n=0)$ and $f_n=\mathbb P_0(T=n)$. Decomposing at the first return and using the <Markov property> gives the <renewal equation>
$$
u_0=1,\qquad u_n=\sum_{k=1}^n f_k u_{n-k}\quad(n\geq1).
$$
For $0<s<1$, define the <probability generating functions> $U(s)=\sum_{n\geq0}u_ns^n$ and $F(s)=\sum_{n\geq1}f_ns^n=\mathbb E_0[s^T]$. Absolute convergence, or nonnegative summation, justifies the convolution identity $U=1+FU$. The <binomial series> gives
$$
U(s)=\sum_{k\geq0}\binom{2k}{k}\left(\frac{s^2}{4}\right)^k=\frac1{\sqrt{1-s^2}}.
$$
Solving the renewal identity gives **the <first-return generating function of the simple symmetric random walk>**
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_0[s^T]=F(s)=1-\sqrt{1-s^2},\qquad 0<s<1.}
$$
As consistency checks, $F(s)\to1$ as $s\uparrow1$, confirming recurrence, while $F'(s)=s/\sqrt{1-s^2}\to\infty$. By monotone convergence of $\sum_n n f_n s^{n-1}$, this last limit is $\mathbb E_0T=\infty$, confirming <null recurrent states>.
